    "heading": "Coffee And A Quick Puzzle",
    "body": "I started my morning with the easy grid, and it was a great way to wake up the brain. The first things that jumped out at me were the single cells with a target sum of six at (0,0) and (0,5). Since I knew the dominoes for today included [4,6] and [6,0], these were the perfect places to start. I put the 6 from [6,0] in the top left corner at (0,0) and the 6 from [4,6] at the top right at (0,5).\n\nMoving down the grid, the sum targets of five were the next big help. At (1,5), I paired it with the 6 above it, which meant that cell had to be a 4 to finish the [4,6] domino. That left (1,4) needing to be a 1 to hit the sum target of five for that little region. Following that logic over to the left side, (1,0) became a 0 to complete the [6,0] domino, which forced (1,1) to be a 5 to meet the other sum target of five. It all clicked into place once I realized the empty cell at (2,1) was just the other half of the [5,5] domino.",
    "medium": {
      "heading": "Thinking Outside The Boxy Grid",
      "body": "The medium puzzle felt like a big step up because of those equals rules. Having three cells in a row at (1,2), (1,3), and (1,4) that all had to be the same value really limited my choices. I spent a few minutes looking at the dominoes [1,5], [1,1], [3,2], [6,0], [4,2], [0,2], [2,2], and [5,2] to see which numbers appeared most often. Since 2 showed up in five different dominoes, I had a feeling it was going to be a popular number in this grid.\n\nI focused on the bottom left where three cells at (2,0), (2,1), and (3,1) also had to be equal. By pairing (3,1) with the empty cell at (4,1), I realized I could use the [0,2] domino if I set those equal cells to 2. This move was a total breakthrough because it helped me place the [2,2] domino right next to it. From there, I worked my way up to the top, using the greater than four and less than four rules to figure out where the 5s and 3s needed to go. It was like a game of musical chairs with numbers!",
    "hard": {
      "heading": "The Double Four Challenge",
      "body": "Today's hard puzzle by Rodolfo Kurchan was a real masterpiece. It used a double-four domino set, which means all the pairs from [0,0] up to [4,4]. The grid was covered in sum targets of zero, which at first felt intimidating, but they are actually huge clues. Every time I saw a 0 target, like at (2,3) or (2,5), I knew exactly what pip value to put there. The biggest challenge was figuring out what the empty cells meant. After a bit of trial and error, I figured out that all those empty cells were actually representing the value 1.\n\nOnce I made the connection that empty meant 1, the whole board started to melt away. I paired the 0 at (3,2) with the 4 at (4,2) to use the [0,4] domino, and then used the [3,3] domino for the two cells at (0,2) and (0,3) that both had sum targets of three. My favorite move was finishing the bottom right corner by placing the [4,4] domino across (4,5) and (4,6). It was so satisfying to see that final domino slide into place without any leftovers. If you got stuck, the trick was definitely focusing on those sum of zero cells first to anchor your search.",
    "tips": "When you see a region with only one cell and a sum target, fill it in immediately since it tells you exactly what one half of a domino must be. Also, keep a physical or mental list of the dominoes you have already used so you do not accidentally try to use the same one twice. If you hit a wall, look for the most restricted area, like a spot where multiple regions touch, as that is usually where the next answer is hiding.",
    "learned": "I learned that empty cells in Pips are not always just zeros or blanks. Depending on the puzzle creator, they can be used to represent a specific value that is missing from the other sum targets, like how they all represented 1s in today's hard puzzle. It is a good reminder to look at the available dominoes first to see what numbers are missing from the targets.\n\nI also noticed how powerful the equals constraints are. In the medium puzzle, having two different areas where three cells had to match really forced the rest of the board to follow a specific path. It turned the puzzle from a math problem into a pattern-matching game, which is a fun change of pace.",
